sses  claiming they weren't providing equal  access but  here's the catch she never actually went  to the businesses she's suing as local  news koat reports  in the case of room's nursery alyssa  says she didn't go inside  something the owner of the nursery told  us too nobody recalls her  nobody alyssa carton is one of many  disabled people  hired to do something called drive-by  lawsuits these are lawsuits which are  filed by the hundreds  where a disabled person gets in a van  and usually just drives down the road  looking for any accessibility issue to  sue over with one goal in mind  settlements from the businesses sued  fox9 reports that  these lawsuits are often served with  immediate settlement offers  typically the lawsuits are served on a  business with an offer to settle  for between thirty five hundred to eight  thousand dollars  but while thousands of dollars may seem  expensive the alternative actually  fighting the case is usually worse even  if you win the legal bill is often more  than the settlement would have been  candyland rolled the dice and ended up  with a fourteen thousand dollar legal  bill  so for a principal you actually ended up  paying fifteen 15 000  but if you're thinking to yourself that  maybe these lawsuits are really just  about fast-paced change  think again many stores who paid out  settlements report that  no actual changes to their stores were  required or ever checked  simply paying the hush money was enough  the settlements didn't require any  repairs  that tells me that these lawsuits are  not about what we claim that they're  about  are they about accessibility for folks  with disabilities  or are they extortion which is it  lawyers are hiring out disabled people  driving them downtown and suing hundreds  of businesses out of time not because  they visit the stores but for ridiculous  reasons like tiny compliance issues  this was ground zero for the technical  issues he said he easily would have  fixed if a person with disabilities had  bothered to strike up a conversation  with him  instead of filing litigation over things  like the toilet paper dispenser which  was off by a few inches  like the urinal which was a few inches  too high and the businesses sued have  two options  settle and pay a few thousand dollars or  fight it and end up paying up to five  figures in legal fees and look this is a  complicated issue  for the disabled people who are involved  who rightfully want  disability access and often believe  they're getting into something honorable  alyssa carton who we met earlier said  she answered a craigslist ad mentioning  civil rights  they said they were looking for somebody  who cared about ada  but in many of these cases things look  less like civil rights and more like a  shakedown  in some the plaintiff who claims to be  disabled is later found  on camera actually to be walking take  for instance this man  who's supposed to be using a walker this  is gerald boytnut  boytnott's name is on 31 separate  lawsuits we watched one day as he  carried his walker  up a flight of icy steps to his home he  could walk again  absolutely incredible what an ada  lawsuit will do for you again walking  out and he's not even the only one  a well-known group who serially sues  local businesses is called  aid advocates for individuals with  disabilities and they just hired this  man  david ritzenthaler he's on hundreds of  lawsuits for eight but arizona reporter  dave biscobing found some holes in his  story  firstly was his claim of not making  money from the organization that hired  him  i get not a cent his lawyer peter  struejnik has a different story does he  get paid anything for this  yes how come your lawyer said he gives  you money they do not give me money  either 100 or 75 goes to ritz inhaler  not only that david ritzenthaler has  another secret he claims to have a  disability walking it's not easy to get  up and out of my chair how do you get  around as a cane  and well i hope it's with a cane bad  days i'm in a wheelchair but later  surprise surprise he seems to be walking  just fine david  also one other thing uh how long you  been walking without a cane  it's another absolute miracle he can  walk although he gets testy when dave  asks about his walker  i don't walk more in a pot in fact  i don't have this one i carry a cane in  the car with me just because  not today i don't have it in here today  now as a reminder that is a small clip  from a full investigation by dave  biscuit  please go watch it local investigative  news really doesn't get enough credit  and i won't give the whole thing away  but  let's just say that wasn't the only time  david ritzenthaler was found to be  walking  now what's the point of all this is it  that the ada is bad  no obviously not it's the lawyers who  are abusing the true purpose of it that  are the problem  and honestly some courts are getting fed  up recently peter storchnick the  notorious lawyer behind david  ritzenthaler was  disbarred and that would have made a  really tidy end to the story the good  guys finally winning and the bad lawyer  getting sanctioned and eventually  disbarred  but the truth is really that neat  because recently strojnic  is back once again filing lawsuits  not for other people because remember he  isn't a lawyer anymore he's filing  wait for it on behalf of himself  and he's filing these lawsuits by the  hundreds this time though  there's no investigative journalist  who's done the work to guide us  so i did some taking personally to find  out what's going on  and what i found will shock you find out  next time  on coffee zolo  [Music]  it's not easy to get up and out of my  of chair while your honey ends up
 

